.TH SETIME 1.SH NAMEsetime \- set the system clock.SH SYNOPSIS\fBsetime\fR[yy mm dd hh mm ss].SH DESCRIPTION\fBsetime\fRsets the system date and time, from six fields on its command line orfrom an answer to its prompt,\fBYYMMDDHHMMSS\fR.\fRIt is a stand-in for OS-9's own\fBsetime\fR;\fR\fBsetime2\fRand\fBsetyear\fRalso handle years after 1999..PPUnder os9exec the clock cannot be set by any program -- the emulatoranswers the request with success and leaves the host's clock alone -- sohere it accepts the time and nothing changes..SH NOTESYour own OS-9 has a\fBsetime\fRof its own; see\fBDOC/README-NAMES\fR.\fR.SH SEE ALSOsetime2, setyear, setimex, date